" :answer-field/choices ["q-67459/selection/aa5e1d4c8b406bf008742dead7232d7f15fdc60dfbf22980ff54a99e3f28e033" "q-67459/selection/210b2d33f43526a85322824318a48f4d3cb50e93fe71351ab11c39f0c73f954c" "q-67459/selection/c8023004c4ef014dd45ec8f183c202671545b0ce8b328147859da3f4f33bd45d" "q-67459/selection/e534fbd071286330a5dccbcf85e401eb638060cb72af386e3aea4b356081987c" "q-67459/selection/b2580ca6b71c6824c839bd7fd87ae287df8086343fbfacc67d938d0c01f1b479"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-67459" :question/id #uuid "5c53384c-4d83-5794-87e6-e6659870884d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67459" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series \n ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2(1)}}{(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2(0)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-67459/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-67459"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40282/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e98ce386-ad06-5e85-9a9f-5398d8037b82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40282/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "b6ae32b0-2901-5a06-b135-c9e2469a7011" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40282/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "bfaca0e9-18da-5f11-b0c7-2c8b07d86b51" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40282/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "79e5acd7-dd7e-5ea8-88ee-185b6a91f49c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40282/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7ed13411-0552-58fe-9b75-e8244ef889f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40282/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf08d0d-3b07-51af-a637-5f9df8c367d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40282/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-40282/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-40282/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-40282/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-40282/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-40282/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40282" :question/id #uuid "3ba97993-eea9-588a-a8e1-7812184f7fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40282" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $1+5+25+125+⋯$\n- $\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$\n- $0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $1+5+25+125+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{7})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.01}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series III converges." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40282/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40282"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124912/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "c59ce3d3-43c6-5524-8cd0-b01523e025ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124912/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c68a442b-2ecc-57ac-a851-f52e0b1bf81f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124912/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "852359ed-8cf2-5bf5-a3f5-621916fc8cb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124912/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "1db5356e-50e1-59b3-bb3e-b2f75abf8053" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124912/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f398900c-0757-548b-931c-76d1a6a4dd7e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124912/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9be666-3755-56fd-b7a4-bfdfe407b825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124912/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-124912/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-124912/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-124912/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-124912/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-124912/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124912" :question/id #uuid "55c1789a-3f1b-5ebc-a34c-eaab270f436c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124912" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $-1+1-1+1-⋯$\n- $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$\n- $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $-1+1-1+1-⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.11}{0.1} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent  because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-124912/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124912"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124906/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e2fe9fa4-c087-561a-9b03-af80dd4596dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124906/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "246fa7bf-24dc-570b-860f-4875fb3c7abb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124906/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7a312761-0d12-5707-8550-79fa40f0f747" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124906/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1545e8be-5b17-5a36-ba02-42d585c8d7ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124906/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "a7c76799-a70c-5584-85ee-1ef29cd2d0c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124906/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f040b7-a861-517f-b2cc-87b018b09d90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124906/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer-field/choices ["q-124906/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-124906/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-124906/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-124906/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-124906/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124906" :question/id #uuid "0860410e-f60a-5b80-a1c8-6a3d047379c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124906" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{0.25{(1.1)}^{1}}{0.25{(1.1)}^{0}} \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$ starts with $n=2.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{3-1}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{3}^{2-1}}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})(\\frac{{2}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124906/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124906"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40277/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7aa5e743-3f97-55c6-b08b-0ca93f9c0dbe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40277/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "34a61084-acd4-589b-8839-64c470216664" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40277/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6367f161-92ea-5f78-9e9e-4fb23c5ec152" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40277/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "2ec4768f-02f9-5a25-811b-e7d96245f0ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40277/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "f0241e30-571a-57e8-85ec-c3da54e0bf25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40277/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7bdef6-6639-5f43-8df8-c7f88578dc0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40277/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-40277/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-40277/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-40277/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-40277/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-40277/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40277" :question/id #uuid "5d6af0ce-2d7e-5e7e-9759-710fbc03ce1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40277" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$\n- $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$\n- $-8+16-32+64-⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-2}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-8+16-32+64-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and II converge." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40277/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40277"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-50506/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "a5cf2cd0-dacb-5072-804c-7e4931260523" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50506/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8c1268ad-3b1f-5f67-8608-04384cdb6a4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50506/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "aa027fd0-1806-51a4-b983-b44bb63fbf6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50506/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "17157c03-4542-532f-a9ca-c898ab12a219" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50506/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "682245a4-841b-57e9-ac3d-21e306e62736" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-50506/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d891d2c0-634f-55a3-9528-6139a95c51eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-50506/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-50506/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-50506/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-50506/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-50506/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-50506/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-50506" :question/id #uuid "f576e449-3f8d-544f-ad07-4e21e895f136" :question/math-academy-id "q-50506" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=5.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{8{(2)}^{2}}{8{(2)}^{1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\frac{{8}^{1+1}}{{16}^{1-1}}}{\\frac{{8}^{0+1}}{{16}^{0-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})}{(\\frac{{8}^{1}}{{16}^{-1}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})(\\frac{{16}^{-1}}{{8}^{1}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{1})(\\frac{1}{16\\cdot 8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series  I and III converge." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-50506/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-50506"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-347229/field-1/e4e9dbe71f63d8d81739fdcbf2df64ccccd4c8f5d299ddc4b1c1f96031adb38e" :answer/id #uuid "a08c2b63-9d2c-5fb8-a648-1fb3fa981f37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347229/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d122179f-1932-53fe-8be2-63639107c3bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-347229/field-1/e4e9dbe71f63d8d81739fdcbf2df64ccccd4c8f5d299ddc4b1c1f96031adb38e" :answer-field/choices ["q-347229/field-1/e4e9dbe71f63d8d81739fdcbf2df64ccccd4c8f5d299ddc4b1c1f96031adb38e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-347229/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "f95d9359-367d-58b0-877e-b28a1a26a24e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347229/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5035434-8efb-5412-b0b1-0c248819ba24" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-347229/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-347229/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-347229" :question/id #uuid "dc4ec5a6-b7f3-5cda-ba58-721e08ead6c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-347229" :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{3}^{8}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{9}^{4}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{k-4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{k-4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{k-4}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{k-4} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{k-4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{k-4}=1$ has the solution $k=4.$ Now, since ${9}^{k-4}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{k-4}<1$ must be $k<4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-347229/field-1" "q-347229/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-347229"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124924/selection/979cb5cdeb49248c1f8d050de218edcdd0dca55fbf1d168e04b05a9cb6b3a6ed" :answer/id #uuid "bb2171e5-28ec-54b5-8a7b-c94c7d7509a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124924/selection/043e0db9b8360a81f0c06a90a4ed5190a27abaf714f95424a11a213f03d51d67" :answer/id #uuid "11de28c4-6734-5cbf-aa46-c4221ef7716c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124924/selection/2cace21d91262d0b054ebe63654a83465db9e200a37b82c689045be7c7238590" :answer/id #uuid "6bb81f44-1ce5-56b9-874f-08a1b68ef5e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124924/selection/02cb95146ace98398c4dd0a9c5f8c85d015ec1ba7a79a0c80f65134922e47b6e" :answer/id #uuid "329bacbe-75a0-50d3-bb78-97fd3c6bb3ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124924/selection/716321a7b47e03c28055000c9d706620e0b49e4f945b0f9ad68575447041ded3" :answer/id #uuid "ccc1d7ac-3d06-5834-b298-e6dbcd219807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124924/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb2a0ad3-7172-5af3-bbae-a6ab9da2e6ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124924/selection/716321a7b47e03c28055000c9d706620e0b49e4f945b0f9ad68575447041ded3" :answer-field/choices ["q-124924/selection/979cb5cdeb49248c1f8d050de218edcdd0dca55fbf1d168e04b05a9cb6b3a6ed" "q-124924/selection/043e0db9b8360a81f0c06a90a4ed5190a27abaf714f95424a11a213f03d51d67" "q-124924/selection/2cace21d91262d0b054ebe63654a83465db9e200a37b82c689045be7c7238590" "q-124924/selection/02cb95146ace98398c4dd0a9c5f8c85d015ec1ba7a79a0c80f65134922e47b6e" "q-124924/selection/716321a7b47e03c28055000c9d706620e0b49e4f945b0f9ad68575447041ded3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124924" :question/id #uuid "f3040a9b-f07a-5c90-9674-eff18efde317" :question/math-academy-id "q-124924" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn-1}}{{100}^{n}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn-1}}{{100}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn}}{{100}^{n}\\cdot 10} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{10}^{k}}{100} & <1 \\\\ {10}^{k} & <100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${10}^{k}=100$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since ${10}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${10}^{k}<100$ must be $k<2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-124924/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124924"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40590/selection/cc7abe76e79e0a38312fc1631959886845fc849375b2517b96e1730832e23060" :answer/id #uuid "d2daebe2-6ccd-5ba6-bc3b-d477dd584e36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40590/selection/716321a7b47e03c28055000c9d706620e0b49e4f945b0f9ad68575447041ded3" :answer/id #uuid "9f19568d-1592-5313-a80e-867ed1e4e289" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40590/selection/2cace21d91262d0b054ebe63654a83465db9e200a37b82c689045be7c7238590" :answer/id #uuid "2eacd0ef-e87c-5bde-97f9-10754c0981e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40590/selection/f80396eee55c2cd83cd13fed325434d99ebb39af1b03c197a3a4d8b78ca96cdd" :answer/id #uuid "203072a5-292e-5d66-b2e2-a3fdb36c2cc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40590/selection/043e0db9b8360a81f0c06a90a4ed5190a27abaf714f95424a11a213f03d51d67" :answer/id #uuid "72006af8-0884-50ae-8230-31a2f35ec785" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40590/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "905900f1-a590-5906-8f97-88410f694965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40590/selection/cc7abe76e79e0a38312fc1631959886845fc849375b2517b96e1730832e23060" :answer-field/choices ["q-40590/selection/cc7abe76e79e0a38312fc1631959886845fc849375b2517b96e1730832e23060" "q-40590/selection/716321a7b47e03c28055000c9d706620e0b49e4f945b0f9ad68575447041ded3" "q-40590/selection/2cace21d91262d0b054ebe63654a83465db9e200a37b82c689045be7c7238590" "q-40590/selection/f80396eee55c2cd83cd13fed325434d99ebb39af1b03c197a3a4d8b78ca96cdd" "q-40590/selection/043e0db9b8360a81f0c06a90a4ed5190a27abaf714f95424a11a213f03d51d67"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40590" :question/id #uuid "ed54a7f7-e913-5b8a-b062-8a7b79cd167c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40590" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn}\\cdot 16}{{4}^{n}\\cdot {4}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(16\\cdot 4){(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }64{(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{16}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {16}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${16}^{k}=4$ has the solution $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Now, since ${16}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${16}^{k}<4$ must be $k<\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40590/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40590"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78490/selection/4c82197522aa41730bdc870a26de19240ac691f38a47d7ced8995bf2a700d5d7" :answer/id #uuid "2ae7667e-cde9-5b73-abc7-f634c3c86a5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78490/selection/59ea6aeebaa1651ee6a61213526a865e60d03b75e80a69d22f2a1ca5b0eff302" :answer/id #uuid "3966187a-4f68-5137-a0b3-c5c205f93706" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78490/selection/043e0db9b8360a81f0c06a90a4ed5190a27abaf714f95424a11a213f03d51d67" :answer/id #uuid "6e176fd3-77ad-588d-b032-de5593b426f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78490/selection/034ea27c8cc247ecd26b63260063842df4125626cadbd2ca8fdff1a991bd0b39" :answer/id #uuid "485e2466-bab6-583d-8937-9cdcaa9ffc28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78490/selection/94140e648bf5d0a55781acfe01ab33b57c2871dd1b4f64ed053653b12a78ea9d" :answer/id #uuid "d56cb2ce-34f7-5626-b702-4694d3f4929c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78490/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "693ba853-1694-52f1-a34d-8fcf382a81b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78490/selection/94140e648bf5d0a55781acfe01ab33b57c2871dd1b4f64ed053653b12a78ea9d" :answer-field/choices ["q-78490/selection/4c82197522aa41730bdc870a26de19240ac691f38a47d7ced8995bf2a700d5d7" "q-78490/selection/59ea6aeebaa1651ee6a61213526a865e60d03b75e80a69d22f2a1ca5b0eff302" "q-78490/selection/043e0db9b8360a81f0c06a90a4ed5190a27abaf714f95424a11a213f03d51d67" "q-78490/selection/034ea27c8cc247ecd26b63260063842df4125626cadbd2ca8fdff1a991bd0b39" "q-78490/selection/94140e648bf5d0a55781acfe01ab33b57c2871dd1b4f64ed053653b12a78ea9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78490" :question/id #uuid "81fee5bd-1991-5407-ab65-90b191c16e7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78490" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}})}^{n}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "We see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{64}{{2}^{k}} & <1 \\\\ 64 & <{2}^{k} \\\\ {2}^{k} & >64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${2}^{k}=64$ has the solution $k=6.$ Now, since ${2}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${2}^{k}>64$ must be $k>6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78490/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78490"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351372/field-1/22814de28a0b1b4f20d5edd62707d192b78050d457a70349795d30d86dfcd7fa" :answer/id #uuid "d09fcad5-7e81-5683-ac5f-6276d370cdb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value ">" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351372/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "68efd871-a33d-5d79-b6cb-f1f1b6cd78c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351372/field-1/22814de28a0b1b4f20d5edd62707d192b78050d457a70349795d30d86dfcd7fa" :answer-field/choices ["q-351372/field-1/22814de28a0b1b4f20d5edd62707d192b78050d457a70349795d30d86dfcd7fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351372/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "72bfe6b0-cdc8-5fd8-bd3d-16c044449988" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351372/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "43761fe4-137b-560a-90f3-85cd13ed7030" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351372/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-351372/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351372" :question/id #uuid "ab273945-3eda-5439-90ad-bb1b762ef6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-351372" :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{3}^{6}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{3-k})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{3-k}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{3-k}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{3-k} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{3-k}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{3-k}=1$ has the solution $k=3.$ Now, since ${9}^{3-k}$ decreases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{3-k}<1$ must be $k>3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-351372/field-1" "q-351372/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351372"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-689"] :question/problem "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{n+1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2+1}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| <1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-691"] :question/problem "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty {3}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^{n}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}}{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-693"] :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(7)}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{28}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{24}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6773"] :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(2)^{kn}}{{4}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3\\frac{{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r| < 1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
